                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_435">Oxford,
                        Oxfordshire, Merton College</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the
                        following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an
                        attribution:</p>
                     <p><q>Cautio m. Henr. abyndon custodis Exposita in cista reed in die S. Wlstani
                           a.d. m. cccc. xxviijo ... et habet sex supplementa [2o folios given]</q>.
                        Also another Abyndon caucio of 1431, and others.</p>
                     <p>To put this rejection in context, the following passage from the Supplement
                        (1987, p. x) is helpful: <q>Users of <title>Medieval Libraries</title> may
                           need to be reminded that although the possession of a book by a member of
                           a religious order entitles that book to be listed under the religious
                           house to which the man belonged, personal ownership of books by members
                           of universities or secular colleges does not qualify them for
                           admission.</q>
                     </p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q>T. Westhaugh</q> (fol. 181v) = Thomas
                     Westhaugh (fl. 1436–1472), fellow of Pembroke Hall</provenance>
               </history>
